>
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="smell-gas">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What do I do if I smell gas?</h2>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Smell gas right now?</strong> Call the National Gas Emergency Service first on <a href="tel:0800111999">0800 111 999</a>. It is free, 24 hours. Then ring us to repair the fault.</p></div>
      <p class="brx-answer">Get everyone out and call 0800 111 999. Do not call us first. The gas emergency service makes the leak safe, and we repair it after.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>No flames or sparks.</strong> Do not smoke, light matches or use light switches, doorbells or plugs.</li>
        <li><strong>Open doors and windows</strong> to let the gas out.</li>
        <li><strong>Turn the gas off at the meter</strong> if you can. Move the lever so it sits across the pipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Get everyone outside</strong>, then call 0800 111 999 from outside the house.</li>
        <li><strong>Call us</strong> on {PHONE} once it is safe. We find the leak, fix it and turn your gas back on.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="who">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Who does what in a gas emergency?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The National Gas Emergency Service makes a leak safe. We are the Gas Safe engineers who then repair it and get your gas back on.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Who to call</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>You smell gas</strong></td><td>National Gas Emergency Service, 0800 111 999, first</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Gas capped and needs repair</strong></td><td>Us, {PHONE}</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Boiler, cooker or hob fault</strong></td><td>Us</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Gas pipe inside the house</strong></td><td>Us, after the leak is made safe</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Faulty gas meter or prepayment meter</strong></td><td>Your energy supplier</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>No gas in the whole street</strong></td><td>0800 111 999</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The gas emergency service does not usually repair the fault. They will often cap the supply to an appliance or the whole home. That is where we come in.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="arrival">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What happens when the engineer arrives?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We find the fault, tell you the price to fix it, and only turn the gas back on once it is safe.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>We listen.</strong> You tell us what happened. We read any warning notice the gas emergency service left.</li>
        <li><strong>We test.</strong> We test the gas pipework for leaks and check each gas appliance.</li>
        <li><strong>We find the fault.</strong> Most faults are found in the first hour.</li>
        <li><strong>You get a price.</strong> We tell you the cost of the repair and any parts. Nothing is done until you agree.</li>
        <li><strong>We fix it or make it safe.</strong> If a part is needed and the shops are shut, we cap off just the faulty appliance. The rest of your gas can stay on.</li>
        <li><strong>Gas back on.</strong> We test again, relight your appliances and check they burn safely.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Gas capped or boiler down?</strong> Call now. No call-out fee, 24 hours a day.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="faults">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which gas faults do you fix?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We work on gas boilers, gas cookers and hobs, and the gas pipework that feeds them. We come the same day, not next week.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Gas leaks and pipework</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>Gas leaks found and repaired after capping</li>
            <li>Gas tested and turned back on</li>
            <li>Damaged gas pipes repaired</li>
            <li>Faulty appliances capped off safely</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Boilers</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>Boiler lockouts and error codes</li>
            <li>Boiler will not light (ignition faults)</li>
            <li>Low boiler pressure</li>
            <li>No heating or no hot water</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Cookers and hobs</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>No gas to the hob</li>
            <li>Burners that will not light</li>
            <li>Cookers and hobs connected or disconnected</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Gas fires and back boilers</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>Old gas fires disconnected and capped</li>
            <li>Back boilers taken out and a new combi fitted</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">For a boiler that has stopped working, see <a href="/boiler-repair-liverpool">boiler repair</a>. Old back boiler? See <a href="/boiler-installation-liverpool">boiler installation</a>. We do not repair gas meters, and we do not service gas fires.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="no-gas">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>No gas supply or a gas leak?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A leak smells of gas. No gas at all is often a meter or supply problem, not a leak. Check these first.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Smell of gas:</strong> treat it as a leak. Get out and call 0800 111 999.</li>
        <li><strong>Lever at the meter:</strong> check it is on. It should sit in line with the pipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepayment meter:</strong> check it has credit. A meter fault is for your energy supplier.</li>
        <li><strong>Neighbours off too:</strong> it is likely a supply fault. Call 0800 111 999.</li>
        <li><strong>Only one appliance:</strong> if the boiler works but the hob does not, the fault is in the hob or its pipe. Call us.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Water leak instead? See <a href="/leak-detection-liverpool">leak detection</a>. Electrical fault? Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/emergency-electrician">Ideal Electrical Solutions is a 24/7 emergency electrician</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="co">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What if my carbon monoxide alarm goes off?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Treat it as an emergency. Turn off gas appliances, open windows, get outside and call 0800 111 999.</p>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Feel unwell?</strong> Headaches, dizziness or sickness can be signs of carbon monoxide. Get into fresh air and get medical help.</p></div>
      <p>Carbon monoxide has no smell. Watch for these signs of a faulty gas appliance:</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>A lazy yellow or orange flame, not a crisp blue one.</li>
        <li>Black marks or soot around the boiler or cooker.</li>
        <li>A pilot light that keeps going out.</li>
        <li>More condensation on windows than usual.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Once it is safe, we check the boiler, cooker or hob for the cause. Every home with a gas appliance should have a working carbon monoxide alarm. Read our <a href="/blog/carbon-monoxide-safety-liverpool">carbon monoxide safety guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="landlords">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What must landlords do after a gas fault?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Get the fault repaired by a Gas Safe registered engineer before the appliance is used again, and keep the paperwork.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>An appliance marked unsafe must not be used until it is fixed.</li>
        <li>Your tenant should have safe heating and hot water back as soon as you can.</li>
        <li>Keep a record of the fault and the repair.</li>
        <li>You still need a gas safety check every 12 months.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We can arrange access with your tenant and send the paperwork to you. If the yearly check is due, we can do the <a href="/gas-safety-certificate-liverpool">gas safety certificate</a> on the same visit for £100.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
